WEBVTT

00:01.190 --> 00:04.980
Let's keep going with our
2008 Calculus BC free

00:04.980 --> 00:05.780
answer questions.

00:05.780 --> 00:07.250
So we're on problem number 2.

00:07.250 --> 00:09.480
And I cut and pasted the table
that they gave for the problem.

00:09.480 --> 00:10.790
And I'll read the
rest of the problem.

00:10.790 --> 00:12.100
And let's see what we can do.

00:12.100 --> 00:17.250
So it says concert tickets went
on sale at noon, t equals 0,

00:17.250 --> 00:19.630
and were sold out
within 9 hours.

00:19.630 --> 00:23.130
The number of people waiting in
line to purchase tickets at

00:23.130 --> 00:26.780
time t, is modeled by a
twice differentiable

00:26.780 --> 00:28.560
function L, L of t.

00:28.560 --> 00:33.350
So this is the number of people
waiting in line at anytime.

00:33.350 --> 00:38.180
And this is noon, 12:00
noon, 1:00 pm, 9:00 pm.

00:38.180 --> 00:40.070
All the tickets are
sold by 9:00 pm.

00:40.070 --> 00:41.710
And they tell us it's
twice differentiable.

00:41.710 --> 00:44.900
So that means that whatever
function we're modeling this,

00:44.900 --> 00:49.020
this L of t, that it's
continuous because

00:49.020 --> 00:50.960
it's differentiable.

00:50.960 --> 00:53.300
And since it's twice
differentiable, we also know

00:53.300 --> 00:56.350
that its derivative is
continuous because the second

00:56.350 --> 00:59.330
derivative exists
at all points.

00:59.330 --> 01:04.140
So it says part a-- let me make
sure I'm not writing too

01:04.140 --> 01:11.440
thickly-- use the data in the
table to estimate the rate--

01:11.440 --> 01:13.740
let me actually just copy
and paste this, I've just

01:13.740 --> 01:15.890
figured out how to do this.

01:15.890 --> 01:17.350
So I figure it doesn't hurt.

01:24.310 --> 01:24.700
There you go.

01:24.700 --> 01:28.350
You probably can't read it.

01:28.350 --> 01:32.690
But it says use the data in the
table to estimate the rate at

01:32.690 --> 01:34.970
which the number of people
waiting in line was

01:34.970 --> 01:37.030
changing at 5:30 pm.

01:37.030 --> 01:39.370
So they don't give us
any data on 5:30 pm.

01:39.370 --> 01:43.980
They give us 4:00 pm and
7:00 pm, or t equals 5.5.

01:43.980 --> 01:45.710
Show the computations that
lead to your answer.

01:45.710 --> 01:47.430
Indicate the units of measure.

01:47.430 --> 01:48.280
So what do they want to know?

01:48.280 --> 01:52.520
They want to know an estimate
of the rate at which the

01:52.520 --> 01:56.220
number of people waiting
in line was changing.

01:56.220 --> 01:59.830
So they don't give us a
continuous function definition.

01:59.830 --> 02:02.200
They just give us a bunch
of sample points of

02:02.200 --> 02:03.620
this function L of t.

02:03.620 --> 02:08.700
So the best estimate I can do,
of the rate at which this L of

02:08.700 --> 02:13.520
t is changing at time 5.5-- and
5.5 is in between these two--

02:13.520 --> 02:15.840
is to just figure out the
average rate of change

02:15.840 --> 02:18.770
between time 4 and time 7.

02:18.770 --> 02:19.860
So how do we figure that out?

02:19.860 --> 02:23.760
Well the average rate of
change is just the slope.

02:23.760 --> 02:24.790
So let's write it.

02:27.810 --> 02:35.760
So we could write average
rate of change or delta

02:35.760 --> 02:40.770
L over delta t at 5.5.

02:40.770 --> 02:42.790
You can write it however you
want, however you think that

02:42.790 --> 02:44.770
the exam graders would
best like to see it.

02:44.770 --> 02:48.130
We could say at time 5.5.

02:48.130 --> 02:49.835
We could say approximately
equals, or whatever.

02:49.835 --> 02:52.140
But it's just going to be the
slope between these two points.

02:52.140 --> 03:00.400
So it's L of 7 minus L of 4,
all of that over 7 over 4.

03:00.400 --> 03:03.210
Rise over run or change in
the value of the function

03:03.210 --> 03:06.930
divided by change in the
independent variable.

03:06.930 --> 03:09.520
L of 7 is 154.

03:09.520 --> 03:11.250
They give us that.

03:11.250 --> 03:16.930
L of 4 is 126.

03:16.930 --> 03:20.790
And we divide it by 7 minus 4.

03:20.790 --> 03:31.880
So that equals 54 minus
126 is 24 plus 26 is 50.

03:31.880 --> 03:32.960
So it's equal to 28.

03:36.880 --> 03:39.910
Right, if this was 2
less, it would be 30.

03:39.910 --> 03:42.660
And 7 minus 4 is 3.

03:42.660 --> 03:44.010
So you could say that
the average rate of

03:44.010 --> 03:45.950
change is 28 over 3.

03:45.950 --> 03:50.550
Or you could write
that as 9 1/3.

03:50.550 --> 03:52.530
And they want us to use
the units of measure.

03:52.530 --> 03:54.450
Indicate units of measure.

03:54.450 --> 03:56.905
So the numerator,
this is people.

04:01.850 --> 04:02.570
And what's the denominator?

04:02.570 --> 04:06.150
It's hours, people per hour.

04:06.150 --> 04:10.290
So my best estimate, or our
best estimate of the rate at

04:10.290 --> 04:12.580
which the number of people
waiting in line was changing at

04:12.580 --> 04:15.410
5:30 pm, which is between these
two points, is the average

04:15.410 --> 04:16.640
slope between these two points.

04:16.640 --> 04:20.890
Which is 9 1/3 people per hour.

04:20.890 --> 04:21.790
That's it.

04:21.790 --> 04:23.180
Let's do part b.

04:23.180 --> 04:29.055
Let me clear all of this so we
have enough space for part b.

04:29.055 --> 04:31.370
And I'll copy and don't
know if you can read it.

04:31.370 --> 04:33.770
But maybe you can.

04:33.770 --> 04:37.735
So doesn't hurt to
just copy and paste.

04:42.510 --> 04:48.060
OK part b says, use a
trapezoidal sum with

04:48.060 --> 04:49.550
three subintervals.

04:49.550 --> 04:51.780
Maybe I can make this
a little bit bigger.

04:51.780 --> 04:54.930
Let me see if I can grow
that a little bit.

04:54.930 --> 04:57.170
I don't want to take up
all the space though.

04:57.170 --> 04:58.850
No that doesn't look good.

05:04.570 --> 05:07.590
OK well I'll read it out loud
in case you can't see it.

05:07.590 --> 05:11.670
Use a trapezoidal sum with
three subintervals to estimate

05:11.670 --> 05:13.810
the average number of people
waiting in line during the

05:13.810 --> 05:16.420
first 4 hours that the
tickets were on sale.

05:16.420 --> 05:18.550
If you want to do this
really fast, you wouldn't

05:18.550 --> 05:19.480
have to graph it.

05:19.480 --> 05:21.500
But I'm about to graph it
because I want you to

05:21.500 --> 05:23.250
understand how to
do this problem.

05:23.250 --> 05:24.770
If you understand it, you
don't have to graph it.

05:24.770 --> 05:26.930
So let's graph some
of these points.

05:26.930 --> 05:29.770
And we really just have
to do the first 4 hours.

05:29.770 --> 05:31.580
Actually let's graph all
of them, just because it

05:31.580 --> 05:37.710
